## Why This Is Asked
Real-world traffic shaping at tenant level requires deterministic routing and easy rollback.

## Key Concepts
- Hash-based per-tenant routing
- Namespace ConfigMaps for feature flags
- Canary percentage control and target routing
- Production fallback and validation endpoints

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: determine if a request should go to canary
const enabled = getConfig('cnf.canary.enabled');
const pct = getConfig('cnf.canary.percentage');
if (enabled) {
  const h = hash(`${tenantId}${path}`) % 100;
  routeToCanary = h < pct;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you implement safe rollback if canary metrics drop?
- How to simulate traffic split in tests and monitor drift?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design tenant-aware rate limiting and observability in CNF gateways, a realistic production concern for multi-tenant platforms used by large companies.

## Key Concepts

- Tenant-scoped policy API (REST)
- Sidecar token bucket limiter with TTL cache
- Real-time policy fetch and rotation
- Central audit/log sink and schema

## Code Example

```javascript
class TokenBucket {
  constructor(capacity, fillRate) {
    this.capacity = capacity;
    this.fillRate = fillRate;
    this.tokens = capacity;
    this.lastRefill = Date.now();
  }
  allow(n = 1) {
    const now = Date.now();
    const elapsed = (now - this.lastRefill) / 1000;
    this.tokens = Math.min(this.capacity, this.tokens + elapsed * this.fillRate);
    this.lastRefill = now;
    if (this.tokens >= n) {
      this.tokens -= n;
      return true;
    }
    return false;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle policy drift during high load?
- What approaches ensure audit integrity and privacy?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes multi-tenant CNF provenance at scale, including per-tenant keys, drift detection, rollback, and observability.

## Key Concepts
- Tenant-scoped key management
- Per-tenant SBOMs and provenance enforcement
- ImagePolicyWebhook + OPA constraints
- Drift detection and tenant-aware rollback
- Observability: metrics, traces, audit logs

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: verify image provenance per tenant
async function verifyProvenance(imageRef, tenant) {
  const sigOk = await cosign.verify(imageRef, { key: `kms://${tenant}-key` });
  const sbom = await fetchSBOM(imageRef);
  if (sbom.tenant !== tenant) throw new Error('Tenant SBOM mismatch');
  return sigOk;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you rotate tenant keys without downtime?
- How would you test drift detection across regions?

## Why This Question Is Asked
This question evaluates understanding of multi-tenant mTLS, dynamic certificate reloading, and sidecar patterns. It tests knowledge of secure-by-default design and how certificate rotation integrates with live production traffic.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant TLS materials and certificate management
- Sidecar dynamic TLS reload mechanisms
- Internal CA API integration and certificate rotation
- Header-based tenant identification (X-TENANT-ID)
- Gateway validation rules and trust models

## Code Example
```go
// Fetch tenant certificate, cache with TTL, and wire into TLS config
func (s *Sidecar) getClientCert(tenantID string) (*tls.Certificate, error) {
    if cert, ok := s.certCache.Get(tenantID); ok {
        return cert, nil
    }
    cert, err := s.caClient.FetchCertificate(tenantID)
    if err != nil {
        return nil, err
    }
    s.certCache.Set(tenantID, cert, certTTL)
    return cert, nil
}
```
